  "hero.kicker": "Computing & Intelligence · Quantum Computing · An Essay from First Principles to the Frontier",
  "hero.title": "What Quantum Computers Can Actually Do",
  "hero.sub": "What an ordinary computer cannot do but a quantum one can,<br>less than the marketing says, and more concrete than the sceptics assume",
  "hero.lead": "The news often says a quantum computer “tries all the answers at once”, so it can compute anything quickly. Where is that sentence wrong? This essay asks just one question: <strong>what can a quantum computer do that an ordinary computer cannot?</strong> We start with one qubit and work up to the experimental record of 2026. The page includes a real quantum-state simulator: up to 10 qubits, with complex amplitudes, standard quantum gates, measurement and noise implemented as in the textbooks. Every figure in each chapter is computed by it on the spot. After reading, you should be able to follow a technology news story about quantum computing and know which numbers to check first.",
  "hero.note": "This page was written by an AI. The simulator is real but small: results up to 10 qubits are computed exactly, and larger scales are extrapolated from formulas. The text says which numbers the simulator computes and which come from the literature. The frontier data in Chapter 11 are dated, and the sources are listed at the foot of the page. Vendor roadmaps and marketing are labelled as claims, not established facts, and this page keeps them apart. The page makes no prediction about when the technology will advance, and it is not investment advice.",

  /* ───── 01 ───── */
  "ch1.title": "One Qubit Is Two Complex Numbers",
  "ch1.law": "The state of one qubit is two complex amplitudes. A measurement gives only one bit, and each outcome occurs with a probability equal to the squared modulus of its amplitude.",
  "ch1.p1": "Picture a coin tossed into the air but not yet landed. You say it is “half heads, half tails”, and you mean only that you don't know yet: it already has a definite face up, you just haven't looked. A qubit is not like that.",
  "ch1.p2": "The state of one qubit is written α|0⟩ + β|1⟩. α and β are two complex numbers (a complex number is a point on the plane; see <a href=\"../../../math/algebra/complex-numbers/\">Complex Numbers · Rotation on the Plane</a>). They are called <strong>amplitudes</strong>, and |α|² + |β|² = 1. Two pairs (α, β) that differ only by an overall phase describe the same state, so only two angles really matter: θ sets how much goes into |0⟩ and |1⟩, and φ is the relative phase between them. The state can be written cos(θ/2)|0⟩ + e<sup>iφ</sup>sin(θ/2)|1⟩. Treat θ as latitude and φ as longitude, and every state becomes a point on a sphere. This sphere is called the <strong>Bloch sphere</strong>.",
  "ch1.model.title": "Prepare one qubit, then measure it again and again",
  "ch1.model.how": "Drag θ and φ to put the arrow anywhere on the sphere, choose a measurement direction, then press “Measure once” or “Measure 100 times”. Each measurement first prepares a fresh copy of the same qubit and then measures it, because measuring changes the state. Watch the measured frequencies approach the probabilities calculated from the amplitudes.",
  "ch1.model.aria": "Qubit state on the Bloch sphere, and counts of measurement results",
  "ch1.ctl.theta": "θ (latitude)",
  "ch1.ctl.phi": "φ (phase)",
  "ch1.ctl.basis": "Measurement direction",
