  "dg.bloch.aria": "Bloch sphere schematic: the north pole is |0⟩, the south pole is |1⟩. The angle between the state arrow and the vertical axis is θ, and the angle it has turned around the vertical axis is φ",
  "dg.bloch.cap": "Figure 1. Bloch sphere (schematic). A qubit's state is a point on the sphere: θ is the angle the arrow makes with the north pole, and φ is the angle it has turned around the vertical axis. The arrow is drawn at θ = 60°, φ = 60°.",
  "dg.bloch.pole": "North pole |0⟩, south pole |1⟩: along Z, the result is definite.",
  "dg.bloch.theta": "θ sets the probability of 0 along Z: cos²(θ/2).",
  "dg.bloch.phi": "φ is the relative phase: invisible along Z, seen along X or Y.",
  "dg.mz.aria": "Mach–Zehnder interferometer schematic: a photon is split by the first beam splitter into upper and lower paths; the upper path passes phase φ; the paths meet at the second beam splitter and reach two detectors. Beside it is the equivalent quantum circuit: H, P(φ), H, measurement",
  "dg.mz.cap": "Figure 2. The interferometer and its circuit (schematic, not to scale). The lower path is |0⟩ and the upper path is |1⟩. Each beam splitter is an H gate, the extra path length in the upper arm is the phase gate P(φ), and the two detectors are one measurement.",
  "dg.mz.src": "Photon",
  "dg.mz.bs": "Beam splitter",
  "dg.mz.phase": "Phase φ",
  "dg.mz.up": "Upper",
  "dg.mz.low": "Lower",
  "dg.mz.d0": "Detector 0",
  "dg.mz.d1": "Detector 1",
  "dg.mz.same": "The same thing as a quantum circuit:",
  "dg.mz.g1": "First beam splitter",
  "dg.mz.g2": "Phase on upper path",
  "dg.mz.g3": "Second beam splitter",
  "dg.mz.g4": "Two detectors",
  "dg.chsh.aria": "CHSH game schematic: the referee sends bit x to Alice and y to Bob. They cannot communicate, and answer a and b. Before the game they share an entangled pair of qubits",
  "dg.chsh.cap": "Figure 3. Who sends what in the CHSH game (schematic). The referee's two questions are random, and there is no communication between the two players. The only thing prepared in advance is the pair of qubits shared before the game, or an agreed strategy.",
  "dg.chsh.ref": "Referee: random x and y",
  "dg.chsh.alice": "Alice",
  "dg.chsh.aliceDo": "Gets x, sends a",
  "dg.chsh.bob": "Bob",
  "dg.chsh.bobDo": "Gets y, sends b",
  "dg.chsh.wall": "No communication",
  "dg.chsh.pair": "Entangled pair, shared in advance",
  "dg.chsh.rule": "Win if a ⊕ b = x ∧ y",
  "dg.amps.aria": "Comparison: three ordinary bits are at this moment just one of eight combinations. A three-qubit state gives each of the eight combinations its own complex amplitude, shown by eight arrows",
  "dg.amps.cap": "Figure 4. How much must be stored for three bits and for three qubits. The arrows are the 8 amplitudes of a real three-qubit state from the simulator: length is the magnitude, and direction is the phase.",
  "dg.amps.classical": "3 ordinary bits: one of these at a time",
  "dg.amps.quantum": "3 qubits: one amplitude per combination",
  "dg.amps.count": "2³ = 8 complex numbers; n qubits need 2ⁿ",
  "dg.dj.aria": "Deutsch–Jozsa circuit: three input qubits start at |0⟩ and one answer qubit starts at |1⟩. Each first passes an H gate, then all go into the black box together. The input qubits pass H again and are measured. Four dashed lines mark steps 0 to 3",
  "dg.dj.cap": "Figure 5. The Deutsch–Jozsa circuit (schematic). The top three lines are the input qubits; the bottom line is the answer qubit. The gold 0 to 3 are the four positions of “how far the circuit has gone” in the model below. The black box is called only once.",
  "dg.dj.oracle": "Black box f",
  "dg.dj.in": "Three input qubits: measured at the end",
  "dg.dj.ans": "Answer qubit: not measured",
  "dg.grover.aria": "Geometry of Grover's search: the state arrow lies in the plane spanned by “target” and “all the other options”. Each round turns it toward the target by 2θ. For N = 64 it is almost vertical after 6 rounds",
  "dg.grover.cap": "Figure 6. Grover's search is a sequence of rotations (drawn to scale for N = 64). The horizontal axis is the uniform superposition of “all the other options”, and the vertical axis is the target. The square of the arrow's projection onto the vertical axis is the probability of measuring the target.",
  "dg.grover.yaxis": "Target",
  "dg.grover.xaxis": "Other {rest} options",
  "dg.grover.scale": "For N = {N}, θ ≈ {th}° (sin θ = 1/√N). Each round turns 2θ toward the target; round {k} reaches {end}°, almost on target.",
  "dg.grover.rule": "Turn past the target and the probability falls.",
  "dg.shor.aria": "Three stages of Shor's algorithm: first, classically choose a base a. Then the quantum circuit: the counting register passes H gates and controls multiplication of the working register by powers of a; an inverse quantum Fourier transform and a measurement give y. Finally, classically, continued fractions read the period r and a gcd is taken",
  "dg.shor.cap": "Figure 7. The three stages of Shor's algorithm (schematic). Only the middle stage is quantum. Thick lines represent groups of qubits: the counting register controls “multiply by a power of a” on the working register, and after the inverse quantum Fourier transform (QFT⁻¹), y is measured. The stages before and after run on an ordinary computer.",
  "dg.shor.s1t": "① Classical",
  "dg.shor.s1": "Choose an a coprime to N",
  "dg.shor.s2t": "② Quantum: find the period",
  "dg.shor.s3t": "③ Classical",
  "dg.shor.s3": "Read period r from y / 2ᵗ by continued fractions",
  "dg.shor.s3b": "Then compute gcd(a^(r/2) ± 1, N)",
  "dg.shor.count": "Counting register: t qubits",
  "dg.shor.work": "Working register",
  "dg.noise.aria": "Noise model schematic: a Grover circuit on three qubits. It starts with one layer of H, then each round has six layers: black box, H, X, multi-controlled Z, X, H. After each layer, a red cross on every qubit marks a possible error location",
  "dg.noise.cap": "Figure 8. Where the model below injects errors (drawn for 3 qubits and 1 round; the model uses more qubits and rounds). Each red cross is a place where an error can occur: one per qubit, after each layer.",
  "dg.noise.orc": "Black box",
  "dg.noise.round": "One round = {L} layers",
  "dg.noise.count": "{n} qubits × (1 initial layer + one round of {L} layers) = {n} × {per} = {tot} places where an error can occur. For n qubits and k rounds, it is n × (1 + {L}k).",
