  "footer": "What Quantum Computers Can Actually Do · <a href=\"../../../\">visual.integ.life</a><br>Simulator: state-vector simulation with complex amplitudes (up to 10 qubits), standard quantum gates, and measurement by the Born rule. Noise is averaged by the quantum-trajectory method (random Pauli errors). See Nielsen and Chuang, “Quantum Computation and Quantum Information”, chapters 1, 4, 5, 6, 8 and 10. Models by chapter: the Bloch sphere and the Born rule; Mach–Zehnder interference and dephasing; the CHSH game (Clauser, Horne, Shimony, Holt 1969); the Deutsch–Jozsa algorithm (1992); Grover's search (1996) and its lower bound (Bennett et al. 1997); Shor's algorithm (1994) and continued fractions; the heuristic complexity of the number field sieve; the break-even point of a quadratic speedup (Babbush et al., PRX Quantum 2, 010103, 2021); the three-qubit repetition code; the error-rate scaling of surface codes (form from Fowler et al., PRA 86, 032324, 2012; the coefficients 0.1 and threshold 1% are the values commonly used in resource estimates since Fowler and Gidney, arXiv:1808.06709, Sec. XV); and the Λ scaling of Willow (Google Quantum AI, Nature 638, 920, 2025).<br>Sources for the frontier data (checked November 2026): Google Quantum AI, Nature 638, 920 (2025), the Willow spec sheet and Google blog (2024-12-09); Ransford et al., arXiv:2511.05465 (Helios; Nature 2026, doi:10.1038/s41586-026-10676-4); IonQ et al., arXiv:2510.17286 and the IonQ press release 2025-10-21; Manetsch et al., Nature 647, 60 (2025); Bluvstein et al., arXiv:2506.20661 (Nature 2025, doi:10.1038/s41586-025-09848-5); Quantinuum press release 2026-10-19 and media coverage; Microsoft Azure Quantum, Nature 638, 651 (2025) and the editors' statement in its peer-review file, with the 2026 challenge (Legg) and reply as reported in the media; IBM blog 2025-06-10; Arute et al., Nature 574, 505 (2019); Pednault et al., arXiv:1910.09534, and the IBM Research blog “On ‘Quantum Supremacy’” (2019) for the 2.5-day figure; Pan, Chen, Zhang, PRL 129, 090502 (2022); Google blog 2025-10-22 (Nature s41586-025-09526-6), Bermejo et al., arXiv:2604.15427; King et al., Science 388, 199 (2025); Tindall et al., Science 392, 868 (2026); Gidney and Ekerå, Quantum 5, 433 (2021); Gidney, arXiv:2505.15917; Webster et al., arXiv:2602.11457; Pappalardo et al., IACR ePrint 2026/2665; Babbush et al., arXiv:2603.28846; Lee et al., PRX Quantum 2, 030305 (2021); Low et al., PRX 15, 041016 (2025); NIST FIPS 203/204/205 (2024-08), HQC selection (2025-03), NIST IR 8547 (2026-10); RSA-250 factorisation (Boudot et al., 2020-02); RSA-260 factorisation (Eric Lu, Cognition company blog, 2026-09); RSA-896 factorisation (Stephen Weis, saweis.net, 2026-09); RSA-270 factorisation (forum post cited in Wikipedia's “RSA numbers” article, 2026-11); Chromium blog 2024-05-23 (Chrome 124); Firefox 132 release notes; Häner and Steiger, SC17 (45-qubit simulation); De Raedt et al., arXiv:2511.03359 (50-qubit simulation)."
